<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red River (Asia)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sông Hồng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red River rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and flows south-east through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> to the Gulf of Tonkin. Its fertile, flood-prone delta, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> stands, was the heartland of the earliest Vietnamese states and of later dynasties, which built great dikes to control it. French expeditions up the river in the 1870s and 1880s led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Tonkin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-river-asia.jpg" alt="Red River (Asia)" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Redriverasiamap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony transformed the region’s infrastructure: the Haiphong–Kunming railway to Yunnan was completed in 1910, the Long Bien bridge across the <b>Red River</b> at Hanoi in 1902, and the Trans-Indochinese Railway from Hanoi to Saigon in 1936.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Đại Việt under the Lê was an agrarian society centred on wet-rice cultivation in the <b>Red River</b> delta and the coastal plains of Thanh Hóa and Nghệ An.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Red River</b> delta, governed under the Tang as the Protectorate of Annam from 679, Tang rule left deep institutional and cultural marks before Vietnam secured its independence in 939.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>French expeditions to the <b>Red River</b> delta in the 1870s and 1880s, against Vietnamese forces and Chinese Black Flag irregulars, led to the Patenotre Treaty of 1884, which made northern Vietnam, Bac Ky, a protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tonkin">French protectorate of Tonkin</a></li><li><blockquote>Dikes on the <b>Red River</b> and a law code strengthened the state.</blockquote><a href="/state/ly-dynasty">Ly dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mac kept Le institutions, holding examinations regularly and promoting trade and crafts in the <b>Red River</b> delta.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-dynasty">Mac dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
